**Read:** [The fluid mechanics of aerosols — Goodarz Ahmadi, Clarkson](../sources/Fluid-Mechanics-of-Aerosols-Ahmadi.lingua#r_175C6Q2GTKPZJJ6XSRHF5979G0), Hydrodynamic Forces, wall effects and nonspherical particles; [Fundamentals of single-particle transport — Kulkarni, Baron and Willeke](../sources/Single-Particle-Transport-Kulkarni-Baron-Willeke.lingua#r_34AB62TA1PZDEMM8QQZ0W3A06B), diameter definitions.

An equivalent-volume sphere preserves volume, not every transport property. A fiber's resistance depends on orientation; an aggregate can have a different effective density and mobility from a compact sphere of the same material mass. Nearby boundaries change the disturbance flow and can greatly increase resistance to approach.

A shape factor or wall correction carries a definition and a range of validity. Combining independently derived corrections by multiplication is an additional approximation, especially when their physical effects interact. The image seen by a microscope may not determine a particle's three-dimensional orientation or porosity.

**Work:** Compare volume-equivalent, mobility-equivalent and settling-equivalent descriptions for a documented nonspherical example. Identify which measured property each preserves. Explain which further evidence would support predicting contact with a nearby surface.

